          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does adherence mean in the context of diabetes treatment, and why does it matter?",
          "answer": "Adherence means sticking with the treatment plan agreed with a clinician. In diabetes, that includes taking glucose-lowering medication as prescribed and keeping up with monitoring and reviews. The retrieved guidance describes this in its note \"Sticking with a diabetes regimen: monitoring, medication and review,\" but it does not give a formal definition.\n\nAdherence matters because diabetes is largely self-managed. The guidance says most decisions that affect the outcome are made by the person between appointments. It also says that adherence to glucose-lowering medication is often imperfect in practice.\n\nThe guidance also explains why people may not stick with treatment. It lists cost, complexity, side effects, low health literacy, depression, and simply forgetting. It says each of these has a different remedy, and that treating non-adherence as a character flaw misses the real causes. Structured diabetes self-management education is described as a core part of care that improves glucose control and quality of life.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not say how much poor adherence affects blood sugar or long-term health, so I can't give specifics on that. If you're wondering about your own treatment or any difficulties with it, a clinician or diabetes educator is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
